{"name":"Gazebo Green","hex":"#76826c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gazebo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A belvedere, either a type of summer-house or a roofed, detached porch-like structure, usually in a yard, park or lawn.","A dismountable shelter comprising a roof or awning supported by poles, and often having removable sides; a canopy tent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gazebo"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gazebo","blocks":[{"text":"A gazebo is a pavilion structure, sometimes octagonal or turret-shaped, often built in a park, garden, or spacious public area. Some are used on occasions as bandstands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/%D0%93%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%A0%D0%90%D0%9D%2C_%D1%81%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80._Moscow%2C_Russia._-_panoramio_-_Oleg_Yu.Novikov.jpg/330px-%D0%93%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%A0%D0%90%D0%9D%2C_%D1%81%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80._Moscow%2C_Russia._-_panoramio_-_Oleg_Yu.Novikov.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazebo","wikidata":"Q961082"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Victorian_Gazebo.jpg/500px-Victorian_Gazebo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Victorian_Gazebo.jpg/1280px-Victorian_Gazebo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Victorian_Gazebo.jpg/500px-Victorian_Gazebo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Victorian-style bandstand gazebo at Fellows Riverside Gardens at Mill Creek Park, Youngstown, Ohio","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVictorian_Gazebo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png/500px-Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png/1280px-Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png/500px-Rainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tent gazebo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARainy_day_at_Camp_Horno%2C_Camp_Pendleton.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg/500px-NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg/1280px-NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg/500px-NYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gazebo is a focal point of the Peggy Rockefeller Rose Garden in the New York Botanical Garden. The garden layout, including the design for an iron-lattice gazebo and surrounding fence, was created by the renowned landscape architect Beatrix Farrand in 1916. Due to iron shortages during World War I, construction of the gazebo and the fence was not completed, and the whole design was later forgotten. In the mid-1980s, Farrand's original plans were rediscovered. David Rockefeller donated $1 million for the completion of Farrand's design, and the garden, including the gazebo and the fence, was finally realized in 1988. It was named the \"Peggy Rockefeller Rose Garden\" after David's wife and NYBG Board member  Margaret \"Peggy\" McGrath Rockefeller.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANYBG_Rose_Garden.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg/330px-Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg/960px-Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg/330px-Spitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpitz_Pavillon_Hinterhaus-3233.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg/250px-Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg/500px-Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg/250px-Slovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASlovakia_Bratislava_947.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg/120px-Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg/250px-Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg/120px-Greek-Style_Gazebo_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten